# commitwork — semgrep path exclusions
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. fixtures/scan-canary/dirty/ plants a command injection and an eval() on
# untrusted input so that the SAST lane can be shown to FIRE. Those are the whole point of the
# fixture — and if they counted toward this repository's own posture, a tree built to produce
# findings would be reported as this repository having them. Two of commitwork's published
# criticals would be its own test data.
#
# THIS IS AN EXCLUSION, NOT A SUPPRESSION, and the difference is that the findings are still
# asserted somewhere. bin/test/scan-canary.test.mjs requires each plant to be physically present
# before any scanner result about it is believed, and the canary run points semgrep at this path
# explicitly. A suppression makes a finding stop existing; this makes it stop being *this scan's*.
#
# fixtures/scan-canary/clean/ is deliberately NOT excluded. It is the negative control: semgrep
# staying quiet about a real file is the only thing that distinguishes "nothing wrong" from
# "nothing scanned", and hiding it would make its silence worthless.
#
# See fixtures/scan-canary/README.md and EXPECTED.json (selfScanExclusions).
fixtures/scan-canary/dirty/

# Two data files whose purpose is to CARRY detection-shaped strings. Neither is code and neither
# ships a credential: labelled-findings.json is the labelled corpus the detection lanes are graded
# against (every secret in it is fabricated, `provenance: synthetic`), and the scan-canary
# EXPECTED.json spells the bidirectional-control payload it asserts the scanners find. JSON has no
# comment syntax, so an inline nosemgrep is not possible; each file is named, not its directory.
monitor/test/fixtures/detection-lanes/labelled-findings.json
fixtures/scan-canary/EXPECTED.json

# Not source this repository owns. semgrep already skips some of these by default; naming them
# keeps the scanned population the same whether or not a default changes underneath us.
reference/
reports/
node_modules/
.claude/
